Description: antinomie: read at most 256 entries of an io_submit array
 The capture of io_submit read nr entries of the iocb pointer array at
 entry, each with its iocb and buffer fields, about three records per
 entry, and the array itself as one field of nr pointers. The kernel
 handles at most the context's nr_events entries, and none for a
 context that does not exist (it fails with EINVAL at once), neither of
 which the recorder can know before the call. io_submit(bad_ctx, 1 <<
 20, array) therefore cost millions of records under the never-drop
 contract before the call could fail.
 .
 The recorder now reads at most ANT_AIO_SUBMIT_MAX (256) entries: the
 array field covers them, and one field arg2.iocbpp[256] with the
 status RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED and no bytes stands for the rest. An nr the
 kernel rejects outright (negative, or more pointers than LONG_MAX
 bytes) is recorded as before.
 .
 Why 256 and not more: all entries are fields of the one Entry record,
 up to four each, and the app assembles a record's fields as its
 packets arrive at a cost that grows with the fields the record already
 holds. A bound of 4096 entries (12,290 fields for the test's reads)
 kept the call in the recorder for minutes under never-drop, the guest
 waiting for the host at every packet. 256 entries keep the record at
 about the 1,025 fields of a writev() of UIO_MAXIOV vectors.
 .
 Found by the 0.9.0 bug hunt (K1-01). No contract change (revision 22).
